[%# The ufw recipe sets `default deny outgoing`, so chrony needs this profile
    to reach 123/udp.  It is an application profile, not a bare `ufw allow`.
    setup-ufw-rules starts with `ufw reset`, which drops every rule on the
    guest.  The ufw target runs after this one, so setup-ufw-rules removes any
    rule that this target adds.  Profiles live in /etc/ufw/applications.d, and
    the reset does not touch that directory.  setup-ufw-rules walks `ufw app
    list` and allows each profile there, out and in.  chrony is a client only
    (port 0 in ntp.chrony.conf.tt), so this recipe needs only the outbound half.

    The name is ntp-client, not ntp.  ufw skips a profile whose section name is
    also a service name in /etc/services.  It says so only as a warning on
    stderr:

        WARN: Skipping .ntp.: also in /etc/services

    Then ufw makes no rule from the profile.  chrony reaches no server, and no
    log that a provision reads shows a failure. -%]
[ntp-client]
title=NTP client
description=Outbound time sync for chrony
ports=123/udp
